		{
			"name":"Babbling Tongues",
			"events":[
				{"name":"Pete Brown matches the beers to the bands","short":"petebr(1)","start":"2026-08-21 12:00","end":"2026-08-21 13:00"},
				{"name":"Sophia Smith Galer in conversation","short":"sophia(1)","start":"2026-08-21 13:15","end":"2026-08-21 14:00","type":"other","artist":"How to Kill a Language: Power, Resistance and the Race to Save Our Word: Sophia Smith Galer in conversation","blurb":"By the end of this century half of the world’s 7000 languages will be gone. Killed by war, climate breakdown, migration, nationalism, or neglect – and losing with it the vital knowledge that they have sustained for centuries. Sophia Smith Galer – inspired by her Nonna’s passing, who spoke an endangered Italian “dialët” – journeyed across the globe to report on this disappearing world. Sophia is joined in conversation with broadcaster Huw Stephens."},
				{"name":"Delyth Jewell in conversation with Will Hayward","short":"delyth(1)","start":"2026-08-21 14:15","end":"2026-08-21 15:00","type":"other","blurb":"Plaid Cymru Deputy Minister for Social Care, Mental Health and Women’s Health in the Welsh Government, Delyth Jewell sits down with journalist Will Hayward to talk about the future of Wales. In May, Plaid Cymru won the Welsh election and are now leading the Welsh Government for the first time ever. Investigative journalist, author and Guardian columnist Will Hayward is widely considered one of the leading voices covering Wales and Welsh politics. He sits down with Delyth to talk about whether Plaid can deliver on their big promises, where Wales goes from here, and how we can build a Cymru that doesn’t just survive - it thrives. Expect a cocktail of serious debate and lighthearted chat about the often misunderstood and underreported world of Welsh politics."},
				{"name":"50 years of Rough Trade in conversation with Jude Rogers","short":"50year(1)","start":"2026-08-21 15:15","end":"2026-08-21 16:00","type":"other","blurb":"The legendary Rough Trade record shop opened in West London in 1976, on the doorstep of punk. 50 years later, they’re still here, with stores in London, Nottingham, Bristol, Liverpool, New York, Berlin and for one weekend, at Green Man. Rough Trade has been about cutting the distance between artists and fans. That’s meant unforgettable in-store gigs, exclusive vinyl collaborations, and nurturing a space where music heads and casual diggers feel equally at home. Swansea-born author, arts journalist and broadcaster Jude Rogers will be in conversation with Rough Trade shops co-owner Nigel House, and Rough Trade A&R executive Paul Jones."},
				{"name":"Stephanie Phillips in conversation w/Jessica Kempson","short":"stepha(1)","start":"2026-08-21 16:15","end":"2026-08-21 17:00","type":"other","blurb":"We Were Here is a reclamation of rock’n’roll for the Black artists who tore up and re-wrote its rules, by Big Joanie guitarist Stephanie Phillips. It promises to rip up our received history of rock’n’roll and alternative music and repopulate it with the extraordinary Black artists and influential figures who truly steered its course from the 1930s to the present day, including vital figures like Sister Rosetta Tharpe, Little Richard, Poly Styrene and beyond. Stephanie is joined in conversation with musician Jessica Kempson."},
				{"name":"Caitlin Moran in conversation w/ Pete Paphides","short":"caitli(1)","start":"2026-08-21 17:15","end":"2026-08-21 18:00","type":"other","blurb":"Join the endlessly excellent Caitlin Moran for a sneak preview of her new book, How To Be Hopeful. One morning, Caitlin lay in bed and realised she had finally reached Peak Despair. The moment where, in books and movies, someone goes on a quest to find…hope. But this is not that kind of book. Leaving social media, eschewing 24/7 news for local newspapers, sitting on buses without headphones, and listening to what people are really saying. Caitlin finds that life can be radically transformed when you rebel against the news cycle and algorithms. You can’t change the world - but you can change your days. And, once you’ve changed your days, maybe you could change the world. Just a little bit. Caitlin is joined in conversation by renowned journalist and author Pete Paphides."},
